  "metadata": {
    "title": "Fixational drift is driven by central neural circuitry",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.6076911",
    "publication_date": "2022-02-14",
    "description": "<p>This repository contains the raw data which was analyzed in the paper &quot;Fixational drift is driven by central neural circuitry&quot;.</p>\n\n<p>Data is arranged in &quot;.xls&quot; files, each file contains single cell recordings, where each sheet within the file contain the horizontal and vertical eye position (first and second columns) during the trial, and the third column contains the spike times recorded from the cell during the task.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Eye position is recorded in units of degrees, and at temporal resolution of 1ms. The spike times in the third column are given in units of ms.</p>\n\n<p>The instantaneous firing in the paper was calculated from the spikes timing as provided in this repository. In order to generate a prediction for the eye position from oculomotor spikes, the spikes times where binned to a resolution of 1ms, in accordance to the temporal resolution of the recorded eye position.</p>\n\n<p>When using this data set please cite the paper <a href=\"https://rdcu.be/cKhNo\">&quot;Fixational drift is driven by diffusive dynamics in central neural circuitry&quot;</a></p>\n\n<p>https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-29201-y</p>",
